// The csvkit conformance package.
//
// This package exists to answer a question the library itself cannot: *does the
// rest of the MoonBit ecosystem agree with an independent reference on the same
// grammar?* It is the executable half of the conformance layer — `csvkit`
// provides the corpus decoding, the comparison rules and the candidate trait;
// this package provides the adapters and the report.
//
// Why it is a package of this module rather than a module of its own
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// A separate module would have to depend on `BenPaoBaMingWangXing/csvkit`, but
// `moon.mod` only accepts versioned *registry* dependencies, and this module is
// not published. Putting the adapters in the same module is therefore what
// makes the verification runnable at all.
//
// The cost of that, stated plainly: the module now names two ecosystem
// libraries as dependencies. They are used by *this* package only. The shipped
// library (`moon.pkg` at the module root) and the CLI do not import them, so
// `csvkit` as a library still depends on nothing outside `moonbitlang/core`
// plus `moonbitlang/x` for the CLI and the differential test.
//
// Scope note: `sikadi123/moonbit-csv` is deliberately absent. It declares
// `supported_targets = "+js+wasm-gc"`, and a module may target the intersection
// of its dependencies' supported targets — so depending on it would stop this
// module from building for `wasm`, which the library itself supports and CI
// checks. That is a real finding about the ecosystem rather than a limitation
// of the harness: the trait below is the only thing a candidate must implement,
// and it can be implemented from any module whose target set permits.
import {
  "BenPaoBaMingWangXing/csvkit",
  "maria/csv_parser" @maria,
  "CJR-zhang/mbitsv",
  "moonbitlang/core/env",
  "moonbitlang/x/fs",
  "moonbitlang/x/sys",
}

pkgtype(kind: "executable")
